__d(function(a,e,t,n,i,r,s){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 Part I (Civics)",chapter_number:25,chapter_title:"Panchayati Raj",estimated_study_time_minutes:45,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Understand what Panchayati Raj is and why it was established in India","Identify the three tiers of the Panchayati Raj system at village, block, and district levels","Describe the structure, membership, and functions of the Gram Panchayat","Explain the role of the Gram Sabha and how it enables direct democracy","Describe the roles of the Panchayat Samiti (Block level) and Zila Parishad (District level)","Understand how Panchayats are funded and how they spend money","Connect Panchayati Raj to the 73rd Constitutional Amendment and Karnataka's implementation","Answer questions using correct vocabulary: Gram Panchayat, Gram Sabha, Panch, Sarpanch, ward"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Basic understanding that India is a democratic country where people choose their representatives","Awareness that India has many villages and rural areas needing local governance","Familiarity with the concept of elections and voting","Knowledge that India has different levels of government \u2014 central, state, and local"],introduction:{why_important:"India has over 6 lakh villages where more than 65% of its population lives. It is impossible for the central or state government to directly manage every village's roads, water supply, schools, and drainage. Panchayati Raj gives villagers the power to govern themselves through elected local bodies \u2014 making democracy real at the grassroots level.",real_life_relevance:"If a road in your village has a pothole, a hand pump is broken, or a school needs a new classroom \u2014 it is the Gram Panchayat that addresses it. Understanding Panchayati Raj helps you know who to approach for local problems and how local governance actually works in India.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka has been a pioneer in Panchayati Raj. It passed the Karnataka Zilla Parishads, Taluk Panchayat Samithis, Mandal Panchayats and Nyaya Panchayats Act in 1983 \u2014 one of the first states to devolve significant power to local bodies even before the 73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992). Karnataka's Gram Panchayats are known as 'Grama Panchayats' and the district-level body is the 'Zilla Panchayat.' The state was among the first to implement reservations for women (33%) and SC/ST communities in Panchayat elections. As of recent years, Karnataka has over 6,000 Gram Panchayats.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapter 24 introduced democratic government and how it represents people. Chapter 25 extends this \u2014 showing how democracy works at the most local level, in villages, through the Panchayati Raj system."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Gram Panchayat",role:"The village-level elected body that governs local matters \u2014 maintaining roads, water supply, sanitation, and implementing welfare schemes"},{name:"Gram Sabha",role:"A meeting of ALL adult voters of the village \u2014 the foundation of village democracy where people directly participate in governance"},{name:"Sarpanch (Mukhiya/Adhyaksha)",role:"The elected head of the Gram Panchayat, who presides over meetings and leads local governance"},{name:"Panch (Ward Member)",role:"An elected member of the Gram Panchayat representing one ward (division) of the village"},{name:"Panchayat Samiti (Block Panchayat)",role:"The middle-tier body at the block level that coordinates between Gram Panchayats and supervises their work"},{name:"Zila Parishad (Zilla Panchayat)",role:"The apex (top) tier of the Panchayati Raj system at the district level, overseeing all Panchayat Samitis in the district"}],plot:["The chapter opens by explaining that India's villages need a system of self-governance \u2014 decisions about local needs should be made by local people.","It introduces Panchayati Raj as the three-tier system of local self-government in rural India, established constitutionally by the 73rd Amendment (1992).","The village is divided into wards (smaller areas). Each ward elects a Panch (ward member) to the Gram Panchayat.","All ward members together form the Gram Panchayat, headed by a Sarpanch elected by the Gram Sabha.","The Gram Sabha \u2014 a meeting of ALL adult voters of the village \u2014 is the foundational democratic body. It meets twice a year and reviews the Panchayat's work.","The Gram Panchayat's functions are listed: maintaining village roads, drainage, water supply, streetlights, primary schools, and implementing central and state government schemes.","The Gram Panchayat earns money through taxes (house tax, market tax) and receives grants from state and central governments.","The middle tier \u2014 Panchayat Samiti (or Block Panchayat) \u2014 covers several villages and supervises Gram Panchayats in its area.","The top tier \u2014 Zila Parishad (District Panchayat) \u2014 covers the whole district and handles larger development projects.","The 73rd Constitutional Amendment made Panchayati Raj mandatory in all states and reserved seats for women (33%) and SC/ST communities.","The chapter uses examples from Karnataka and other states to show how Panchayati Raj works in practice."],moral:"Democracy is most meaningful when it reaches the smallest village. Panchayati Raj empowers ordinary villagers to govern themselves, solve their own problems, and hold their representatives accountable. Local governance is the foundation of national democracy."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Three Tiers of Panchayati Raj",simple_explanation:"Panchayati Raj has three levels: village level (Gram Panchayat), block level (Panchayat Samiti), and district level (Zila Parishad). Each level handles different kinds of work.",detailed_explanation:"The 73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992) established a uniform three-tier Panchayati Raj system across India: Tier 1 \u2014 Gram Panchayat (village level): covers one or a group of villages; handles day-to-day local administration. Tier 2 \u2014 Panchayat Samiti / Block Panchayat (block level): covers a group of villages (a block); supervises Gram Panchayats, coordinates block-level development work. Tier 3 \u2014 Zila Parishad / District Panchayat (district level): covers the entire district; oversees all Panchayat Samitis; handles large infrastructure projects like secondary schools, district roads, hospitals. Each tier is elected separately by voters. The three tiers together form a complete pyramid of rural local self-government \u2014 from individual villages up to the entire district.",real_life_example:"Think of it like your school structure: your classroom has a class monitor (Gram Panchayat level), the school has a Head Boy/Girl or Student Council (Panchayat Samiti level), and the school principal oversees everything (Zila Parishad level). Each level handles responsibilities appropriate to its scope.",karnataka_example:"In Karnataka, the three tiers are: Grama Panchayat (village level), Taluk Panchayat (block/taluk level), and Zilla Panchayat (district level). Karnataka has 30 Zilla Panchayats (one per district), approximately 176 Taluk Panchayats (one per taluk), and over 6,000 Grama Panchayats. Karnataka was among the first states to set up this three-tier system, having passed the Zilla Parishads Act in 1983 \u2014 nearly a decade before the 73rd Amendment made it mandatory nationally.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse the three tiers and their names. Remember: VILLAGE = Gram Panchayat (smallest, most local). BLOCK = Panchayat Samiti (middle level). DISTRICT = Zila Parishad (largest, district-wide). Also, students sometimes think the Gram Sabha and Gram Panchayat are the same \u2014 they are not. Gram Panchayat = the elected body (a small group of representatives). Gram Sabha = the meeting of ALL adult voters of the village.",memory_tip:"Three tiers, three sizes: GP (small \u2014 village), PS (medium \u2014 block), ZP (large \u2014 district). Or use the pyramid: village at the base, district at the top. Bottom-up democracy: smallest village \u2192 block \u2192 district."},{concept_name:"Gram Panchayat and Gram Sabha",simple_explanation:"The Gram Panchayat is the elected committee that runs the village. The Gram Sabha is the meeting of ALL voters of the village \u2014 where every adult has a voice.",detailed_explanation:"Gram Panchayat: The village is divided into wards \u2014 small electoral divisions. Each ward elects one representative called a Panch (ward member). All Panchs together form the Gram Panchayat. The Sarpanch is the head of the Gram Panchayat \u2014 elected directly by voters of the entire village (or by the Panchs, depending on state rules). The Gram Panchayat meets regularly (usually monthly) to discuss and decide on village issues. It has powers to levy local taxes, spend allocated funds, and implement government schemes. Gram Sabha: This is the most powerful democratic body at the village level. It consists of ALL registered voters of the village. The Gram Sabha meets at least twice a year. It reviews the Gram Panchayat's work and accounts, approves plans and budgets, selects beneficiaries for welfare schemes, and can question the Sarpanch and Panchs. The Gram Sabha represents direct democracy \u2014 every adult voter participates directly, unlike the representative democracy of the Gram Panchayat.",real_life_example:"Imagine your colony has a Residents' Welfare Association (RWA) committee of 10 elected members who manage the colony's facilities \u2014 that is like the Gram Panchayat. Twice a year, ALL residents are invited to a general meeting to review what the committee did and approve future plans \u2014 that is like the Gram Sabha. The committee works day-to-day, but the general assembly has ultimate authority.",karnataka_example:"In Karnataka, the Gram Sabha is called 'Grama Sabha.' Karnataka law mandates that Grama Sabhas must be held at least four times a year (more than the national minimum of twice). Karnataka's Gram Sabhas are known for active women's participation, especially in districts like Dharwad and Mysuru where Mahila Gram Sabhas (women-only Gram Sabhas) are held to ensure women's voices are heard separately. The Karnataka Panchayat Raj Act requires that all beneficiary selection for government schemes (like housing, scholarships) be done transparently at the Gram Sabha.",common_mistakes:"The most common mistake: students think Sarpanch is always elected directly by all village voters. In some states, the Sarpanch is elected by the Panchs from among themselves; in others (including some in Karnataka), the Sarpanch is directly elected by village voters. Also: Gram Sabha is NOT a small committee \u2014 it is every adult voter. Many students think it is just another group of officials.",memory_tip:"GP = Government body (small elected group \u2014 the Panchayat workers). GS = General Sabha (everyone \u2014 all adult voters meet). Panchayat = the ELECTED FEW. Sabha = the ENTIRE VILLAGE meeting. P for Panchayat = 5 (Panch = five in Sanskrit \u2014 historically five elders). S for Sabha = ALL."},{concept_name:"Functions and Finances of Gram Panchayat",simple_explanation:"The Gram Panchayat is responsible for basic services in the village \u2014 roads, water, schools, drainage, sanitation \u2014 and it earns money through local taxes and government grants to do this work.",detailed_explanation:"Functions of Gram Panchayat \u2014 the GP is responsible for: (1) Infrastructure \u2014 construction and maintenance of village roads, drains, village wells and hand pumps, streetlights, public buildings. (2) Health and Sanitation \u2014 maintaining cleanliness, building and maintaining public toilets, operating health centres. (3) Education \u2014 maintaining primary school buildings, supporting Mid-Day Meal programmes, managing anganwadi centres. (4) Welfare Scheme Implementation \u2014 identifying beneficiaries for MGNREGA (job guarantee), PM Awas Yojana (housing), PDS (public distribution/ration shops), scholarships. (5) Civic Registration \u2014 issuing birth and death certificates. Sources of Income for Gram Panchayat: Own tax revenue (house tax, water tax, market/mela tax, vehicle tax at the village level). Grants from state government (State Finance Commission allocations). Grants from central government (Central Finance Commission, scheme funds). The GP must maintain accounts, conduct social audits, and present all expenditure to the Gram Sabha.",real_life_example:"If the hand pump in your village stops working, it is the Gram Panchayat's responsibility to get it repaired \u2014 they use their fund allocation for this. If a family in your village needs a house under PM Awas Yojana, their name must be approved at the Gram Sabha meeting, processed by the Gram Panchayat, and submitted to the block office. Every step involves the GP.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's MGNREGA (Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act) implementation is largely managed through Gram Panchayats. Karnataka's GP accounts are subject to Social Audit \u2014 a public process where the GP's expenditure is read aloud at a Gram Sabha and villagers can question each entry. The Rajiv Gandhi Panchayat Sashaktikaran Abhiyan (RGPSA) provided funds to Karnataka's GPs for building their own panchayat bhavanas (offices). Karnataka's Grama One initiative (2022 onwards) provides 90+ government services at the Gram Panchayat level \u2014 making the GP a one-stop service centre.",common_mistakes:"Students often think the Gram Panchayat only repairs roads. In fact, it manages a wide range of functions \u2014 health, education, welfare, sanitation, and civil registration. Also, students sometimes think the GP gets all its money from the government. In fact, the GP is supposed to collect its OWN taxes (house tax, water tax) to be financially independent \u2014 though in practice many GPs are heavily dependent on government grants.",memory_tip:"GP functions = RIEWS: Roads, Infrastructure, Education, Water, Sanitation. Or think: 'What does a village need every day?' The GP provides it. Income = TGG: Taxes (own), Government grants (state), Government grants (centre)."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Panchayati Raj",meaning:"The system of local self-government in rural India, organised at three levels \u2014 village (Gram Panchayat), block (Panchayat Samiti), and district (Zila Parishad). Established constitutionally by the 73rd Amendment in 1992."},{term:"Gram Panchayat",meaning:"The elected local government body at the village level. It consists of elected ward members (Panchs) and is headed by the Sarpanch. Responsible for managing basic village services and implementing government schemes."},{term:"Gram Sabha",meaning:"A meeting of all adult voters (18 years and above) of a village. The Gram Sabha is the most fundamental democratic body \u2014 it reviews the Gram Panchayat's work, approves plans, and selects beneficiaries for welfare schemes."},{term:"Sarpanch",meaning:"The elected head (president/chairperson) of the Gram Panchayat. Also called Mukhiya in some states. The Sarpanch presides over Gram Panchayat meetings and represents the village in dealings with higher authorities."},{term:"Panch",meaning:"An elected ward member of the Gram Panchayat. Each ward (a division of the village) elects one Panch. The Panchs collectively form the Gram Panchayat."},{term:"Ward",meaning:"A small electoral division of a village or town. Each ward elects one representative to the local government body. Dividing a village into wards ensures representation from all parts of the village."},{term:"Panchayat Samiti",meaning:"The middle-tier body of Panchayati Raj at the block level. It coordinates and supervises the Gram Panchayats in its area. Also called Block Panchayat or Taluk Panchayat (Karnataka)."},{term:"Zila Parishad",meaning:"The apex (highest) tier of Panchayati Raj at the district level. It oversees all Panchayat Samitis in the district and handles large development projects. Called Zilla Panchayat in Karnataka."},{term:"73rd Constitutional Amendment",meaning:"A 1992 amendment to India's Constitution that made Panchayati Raj mandatory in all states, established the three-tier system, mandated elections every 5 years, and reserved seats for women (33%) and SC/ST communities."},{term:"Social Audit",meaning:"A public process where the Gram Panchayat's income and expenditure records are read aloud at a Gram Sabha meeting. Villagers can question each expenditure, ensuring transparency and accountability in local government."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Comprehension",question:"A village has 5 wards. Explain how the Gram Panchayat is formed and how it is different from the Gram Sabha.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Ward elections \u2014 Each of the 5 wards holds an election. Adult voters of each ward elect one representative called a Panch. So 5 wards = 5 Panchs elected.","Step 2: Gram Panchayat formation \u2014 The 5 elected Panchs together form the Gram Panchayat. A Sarpanch is elected to head it (by all village voters or by the Panchs, depending on the state).","Step 3: Gram Panchayat's role \u2014 This small elected group meets regularly to manage village services \u2014 roads, water, sanitation \u2014 and implement government schemes.","Step 4: Gram Sabha \u2014 The Gram Sabha is different: it consists of ALL adult voters of the village (not just 5 elected Panchs). It meets twice a year (minimum) to review what the Gram Panchayat did and approve future plans.","Conclusion: Gram Panchayat = 5 elected representatives running the village day-to-day. Gram Sabha = all adult voters meeting twice a year to oversee and direct the Gram Panchayat. The Sabha has ultimate democratic authority over the Panchayat."]},{example_number:2,type:"Vocabulary in context",question:"Use the term 'Social Audit' in a sentence and explain why it is important for democracy.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Recall meaning \u2014 Social Audit is a public process where the Gram Panchayat's income and expenditure are read aloud at the Gram Sabha so villagers can question each entry.","Step 2: Connect to democracy \u2014 Democracy requires that elected representatives are accountable to the people who elected them. Social Audit is the mechanism through which villagers hold their Gram Panchayat accountable.","Step 3: Write sentence: 'A Social Audit was conducted at the Gram Sabha where the Sarpanch read out all expenditures from the past year \u2014 villagers immediately questioned a payment for road repairs that no one had seen being done, showing the power of Social Audit in preventing corruption at the local level.'"]},{example_number:3,type:"Moral application",question:"The hand pump in your village has been broken for three months. No one has repaired it. What steps should villagers take using the Panchayati Raj system?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Identify responsibility \u2014 Maintenance of village hand pumps is the Gram Panchayat's responsibility. The local Panch (ward member) is the first point of contact.","Step 2: Approach the Panch \u2014 Villagers should formally inform their ward's Panch about the broken pump and demand action.","Step 3: Raise at Gram Panchayat meeting \u2014 If the Panch does not act, the matter should be raised at the next Gram Panchayat meeting (monthly) by any ward member.","Step 4: Raise at Gram Sabha \u2014 If the Gram Panchayat meeting produces no results, the issue should be raised at the next Gram Sabha meeting where ALL voters can collectively demand accountability from the Sarpanch and Panchs.","Conclusion: Panchayati Raj gives villagers a structured democratic process to resolve local problems. The key is knowing which body to approach \u2014 Panch \u2192 Gram Panchayat meeting \u2192 Gram Sabha \u2014 escalating to the appropriate level until the problem is resolved."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Three Tiers of Panchayati Raj",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Name the three tiers of the Panchayati Raj system from village to district level.",a:"Tier 1 (village): Gram Panchayat. Tier 2 (block): Panchayat Samiti. Tier 3 (district): Zila Parishad."},{type:"Recall",q:"Which Constitutional Amendment made Panchayati Raj mandatory across India?",a:"The 73rd Constitutional Amendment, passed in 1992, made the three-tier Panchayati Raj system mandatory in all states."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why is Panchayati Raj described as 'grassroots democracy'?",a:"Panchayati Raj is called grassroots democracy because it brings democratic governance to the most basic level \u2014 the village. Instead of all decisions being made in Delhi or the state capital, local people elect their own representatives to solve local problems. The 'grass roots' are the ordinary people in villages, and this system gives them direct power."},{type:"Understanding",q:"What is the significance of reserving seats for women in Panchayat elections?",a:"Reserving at least 33% seats for women ensures that women \u2014 who make up half the population but were historically excluded from governance \u2014 can participate in and lead local government. It builds women's political capacity, brings women's needs (water, health, safety) into decision-making, and challenges gender inequality in leadership."},{type:"Application",q:"Your village wants to build a community hall. Which body should take this decision and how?",a:"The Gram Panchayat should take this decision. The process: the Gram Panchayat discusses the proposal at a meeting; it checks if funds are available (from taxes or government grants); it presents the plan at the Gram Sabha for approval by all voters; and once approved, the GP tenders and supervises construction. A Gram Sabha approval ensures the whole village supports the use of funds for this purpose."}]}],key_takeaways:["Panchayati Raj is India's system of rural local self-government, established at three levels: Gram Panchayat (village), Panchayat Samiti (block), and Zila Parishad (district).","The 73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992) made Panchayati Raj mandatory across India.","The village is divided into wards; each ward elects a Panch; all Panchs form the Gram Panchayat headed by the Sarpanch.","The Gram Sabha consists of ALL adult voters of the village and is the most fundamental democratic body.","Gram Sabha meets at least twice a year to review the Gram Panchayat's work, approve budgets, and select welfare scheme beneficiaries.","Gram Panchayat functions: roads, drainage, water supply, sanitation, primary schools, anganwadis, MGNREGA, PM Awas Yojana implementation.","Gram Panchayat income: own taxes (house, water, market) + state government grants + central government grants.","33% of Panchayat seats are reserved for women; seats are also reserved for SC and ST communities.","Karnataka has over 6,000 Gram Panchayats, 176 Taluk Panchayats, and 30 Zilla Panchayats \u2014 one of the strongest Panchayati Raj systems in India.","Social Audit at Gram Sabha ensures transparency and accountability in Gram Panchayat expenditures."],mind_map:{root:"Panchayati Raj",branches:[{branch:"Three Tiers",sub:["Gram Panchayat \u2014 village level","Panchayat Samiti \u2014 block level","Zila Parishad \u2014 district level"]},{branch:"Gram Panchayat Structure",sub:["Ward \u2192 Panch (elected)","All Panchs = Gram Panchayat","Sarpanch = Head of GP","Gram Sabha = ALL adult voters"]},{branch:"Functions of GP",sub:["Roads, drainage, water supply","Health, sanitation, streetlights","Primary schools, anganwadis","Implement MGNREGA, PM Awas, PDS"]},{branch:"GP Finances",sub:["Own taxes: house, water, market","State government grants","Central government grants","Social Audit for accountability"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["Karnataka \u2014 pioneer in Panchayati Raj (1983 Act)","6000+ Gram Panchayats","Grama One \u2014 90+ services at GP level","33% women's reservation implemented"]}]},revision_notes:"Panchayati Raj is India's system of rural local self-government established by the 73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992). It has three tiers: Gram Panchayat (village level), Panchayat Samiti/Taluk Panchayat (block level), and Zila Parishad/Zilla Panchayat (district level). At the village level, the village is divided into wards; each ward elects a Panch; all Panchs together form the Gram Panchayat, headed by the Sarpanch. The Gram Sabha \u2014 consisting of ALL adult voters of the village \u2014 meets at least twice a year to review the Gram Panchayat's work, approve plans, and select beneficiaries for welfare schemes. The Gram Panchayat manages roads, water supply, drainage, sanitation, primary schools, and implements government schemes like MGNREGA and PM Awas Yojana. It earns money through local taxes and government grants. Social Audit at the Gram Sabha ensures transparency. The 73rd Amendment mandated 33% seat reservation for women and reservations for SC/ST communities. Karnataka is a pioneer in Panchayati Raj \u2014 it passed the Zilla Parishads Act in 1983, has 6000+ Gram Panchayats, and the Grama One initiative provides 90+ services at the GP level. Key terms: Gram Panchayat, Gram Sabha, Sarpanch, Panch, ward, Panchayat Samiti, Zila Parishad, Social Audit.",must_remember_facts:["Panchayati Raj has three tiers: Gram Panchayat (village), Panchayat Samiti (block), Zila Parishad (district).","73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992) made Panchayati Raj mandatory across India.","Village is divided into wards; each ward elects a Panch; all Panchs form the Gram Panchayat.","Sarpanch = head of Gram Panchayat. Panch = elected ward member.","Gram Sabha = meeting of ALL adult voters of the village (not just elected members).","Gram Sabha meets minimum twice a year; in Karnataka it meets four times a year.","GP functions: roads, water, sanitation, schools, MGNREGA, PM Awas Yojana, birth/death certificates.","GP income sources: own taxes (house tax, water tax) + state grants + central grants.","73rd Amendment reserved 33% seats for women and seats for SC/ST in Panchayat elections.","Karnataka passed Zilla Parishads Act 1983 \u2014 a pioneer. Has 6000+ Gram Panchayats, 176 Taluk Panchayats, 30 Zilla Panchayats."]}},2565,[]);